<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio José de Sucre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio José de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Sucre</a> was a Venezuelan general and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s closest lieutenant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a>. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pichincha">Battle of Pichincha</a> in 1822, freeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, and the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho">Battle of Ayacucho</a> in 1824, which ended Spanish power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. He became the first constitutional president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> but resigned in 1828 and was assassinated in 1830.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre.jpg" alt="Antonio José de Sucre" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arturo Michelena · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_Jos%C3%A9_de_Sucre._Michelena,_Arturo._1895,_Legislative_Palace,_La_Paz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Antonio José de Sucre</b>’s victory at the Battle of Pichincha in May 1822 brought in Quito, and Bolívar annexed the port of Guayaquil in July 1822, shortly before meeting José de San Martín there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Battle of Ayacucho in December 1824, <b>Antonio José de Sucre</b> advanced into Upper Peru, where the last royalist commander, Pedro Antonio Olañeta, was killed at Tumusla in April 1825.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Quito’s creole elite formed one of the earliest Spanish American juntas on 10 August 1809, but royalist forces suppressed it, and independence came only with the campaign of <b>Antonio José de Sucre</b>, whose victory at the Battle of Pichincha on 24 May 1822 freed Quito.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His Colombian and Peruvian army won the Battle of Junín in August 1824, and <b>Antonio José de Sucre</b> ended Spanish power at the Battle of Ayacucho on 9 December 1824.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Viceroy José de la Serna withdrew to the highlands, where the royalists held out until Simón Bolívar's forces won at Junín in August 1824 and <b>Antonio José de Sucre</b> defeated and captured La Serna at the Battle of Ayacucho on 9 December 1824.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Antonio José de Sucre</b> became its first president.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-6">6 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
